---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SideMark from '../../components/SideMark.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="Proofs — Andrew Shiau"
  description="A skill that renders every design option side by side, in the real system, so you pick by looking."
>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="back"><a href="/">← Index</a></div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid">
    <div class="c1-10">
      <SideMark href="/work/proofs/" />
      <h1>Proofs</h1>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="grid">
    <p class="dek c1-8" style="margin-top:var(--unit)">
      A skill for design decisions with no obvious answer. Instead of describing the
      options in prose, it renders every one for real — in the actual typeface,
      tokens, and grid it will ship in — so you choose by looking. A proof, in the
      print sense: a trial pull you check before you commit.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="grid">
    <div class="c1-8" style="margin-top:calc(var(--unit)*1.5)">
      <p>
        You can't see prose. A byline treatment, a type ramp, a masthead — the
        differences are visual and small, and the only honest way to decide is to
        put the candidates next to each other and use your eyes.
      </p>
      <p>
        I chose this site's layout that way. Both sheets below are the real ones I
        used, not reconstructions.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid sect">
    <!-- a full-width embed follows, not a paragraph — head flush left (option 02) -->
    <div class="c1-12"><h2>This index</h2></div>
  </div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="c1-8" style="margin-top:var(--unit)">
      <p>
        Three poster compositions on the 12-column grid, rendered live. I picked A,
        the calendar scatter, and shipped it — then took it out. It composed well on
        the sheet and indexed badly on the real page: two sections using the same
        device, neither list ever whole in view. The index you're reading is a ruled
        list instead. C survives as the staircase the links use.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="grid">
    <figure class="embed c1-12" style="margin-top:var(--unit)">
      <div class="esrc">
        <a href="/demo/index-layouts/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open the proof sheet full-screen</a>
      </div>
      <div class="embed-frame">
        <iframe src="/demo/index-layouts/index.html" title="A live proof sheet of index layouts" loading="lazy"></iframe>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        <a href="/demo/index-layouts/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open full-screen</a>
        — three index layouts, rendered live, on the real grid. A shipped, then lost
        to the live page.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="grid sect">
    <!-- full-width embed again — head flush left, not .shead -->
    <div class="c1-12"><h2>A harder one</h2></div>
  </div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="c1-8" style="margin-top:var(--unit)">
      <p>
        My recipe archive needed a layout for a wide screen: a 26-column split with
        the section names hung out in a margin. Six open questions, twenty options,
        every frame rendered at a width it actually ships at — 1512, 1792, 2560, and
        390 for a phone.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is round two, so what the first round settled sits in a band at the top
        marked locked. A sheet that reopens a decision you already made is how you
        spend a week choosing a gutter.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="grid">
    <figure class="embed c1-12" style="margin-top:var(--unit)">
      <div class="esrc">
        <a href="/demo/recipes-ultrawide/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open the second proof sheet full-screen</a>
      </div>
      <div class="embed-frame">
        <iframe src="/demo/recipes-ultrawide/index.html" title="A live proof sheet of ultrawide layouts for the recipe archive" loading="lazy"></iframe>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        <a href="/demo/recipes-ultrawide/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open full-screen</a>
        — twenty options for the recipe archive's wide layout, at their real widths.
        Round 2, July 2026, as it stood.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="grid sect">
    <div class="facts">
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Form</div>
        <div class="ftx">
          One self-contained HTML page: every option rendered in the real design
          system, labelled, with a one-line rationale. Open it, pick a number.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">The rule</div>
        <div class="ftx">
          The first option is always what's live today, so you read the rest
          against it. A few distinct options beat a dozen near-duplicates.
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="frow">
        <div class="fm">Rounds</div>
        <div class="ftx">
          A second sheet opens with what the first one settled, marked
          <b>locked</b>. Decisions close; only the open questions get frames.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
